// STL in c++ is a standard template library that provides a set of data structures and algorithms for working with collections of elements.
// It is a collection of generic classes and functions that can be used to perform various operations on collections of elements, such as sorting, searching, and manipulating elements.


// basic components of STL include:
// 1. Containers: These are data structures that store collections of elements. Examples include vector
, list, deque, set, map, etc.
// 2. Algorithms: These are functions that perform operations on collections of elements, such as
    sorting, searching, and manipulating elements. Examples include sort, find, copy, etc.
// 3. Iterators: These are objects that allow you to iterate over collections of elements. Examples include
    vector<int>::iterator it;
    list<string>::iterator lit;
    etc.
// 4. Function objects: These are objects that can be used as functions. Examples include
    greater<int>, less<string>, etc.

// STL is widely used in C++ programming and is an essential part of the language. It provides a powerful and efficient way to work with collections of elements, making it easier to write clean and efficient code.